The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (!adis->ops->write && !adis->ops->read && !adis->ops->reset)
Reason:
This statement dereferences `adis->ops` to access its members (`write`, `read`, `reset`) without first verifying that `adis->ops` itself is not NULL. Since `adis` is zero-initialized (e.g., from `devm_iio_device_alloc()`), `adis->ops` is NULL in drivers that do not set a custom ops struct, causing a NULL pointer dereference.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!adis->ops)
	adis->ops = &adis_default_ops;
else if (!adis->ops->write || !adis->ops->read || !adis->ops->reset)
	return -EINVAL;
Reason:
The fix first checks whether `adis->ops` is NULL. If it is, the default operations (`adis_default_ops`) are assigned. Otherwise, the code then safely checks that each individual function pointer (`write`, `read`, `reset`) is non-NULL by dereferencing `adis->ops` only after confirming it is valid. This prevents the NULL pointer dereference while maintaining the original validation logic.

